insert into public.activities (id, chapter_id, title, type, description, content, estimated_minutes, sort_order, status)
values
('intro','cia-triad','Introduction to cybersecurity','lesson','Connect assets, threats and controls.',
  '{"objectives":["Distinguish an asset, a threat event, a weakness and a control.","Write a risk statement that links evidence to a business consequence."],"markdown":"## Metaphor: a café\n\nCybersecurity is like running a café with a stockroom, a till and an order board. The stockroom needs a lock so only authorised staff can enter. The order board must be accurate so the kitchen prepares the right meals. The till has to work during the lunch rush. Each protection serves a different business need.\n\n## Fictional scenario: a repair shop\n\nA repair shop keeps customer contact details in a shared spreadsheet. The **asset** is the customer data. The **weakness** is that everyone can share the file externally. The **threat event** is someone sending the link to the wrong recipient. The **consequence** is that a stranger could read customer contact details. A **control** is to disable public links and give sharing permissions only to people who need them.\n\nWrite the risk this way: *Because the file allows public links, an accidental share could expose customer contact information to someone outside the business.*\n\n| What you know | What you would check |\n| --- | --- |\n| The example says anyone can share the file. | Review the actual sharing configuration and a sample of permissions. |\n| The file contains customer details. | Confirm what fields it stores and whether each field is needed. |\n| Disclosure is possible. | Check activity logs before claiming it actually happened. |\n\n## True incident: Medibank, Australia, 2022\n\nThe Australian privacy regulator’s court filing describes a cyberattack in which personal and health information was accessed and subsequently published online. The filing puts the number of people exposed to risk at approximately **9.7 million**. Sensitive details can be used for extortion and identity fraud, and their disclosure can cause distress even when a person suffers no financial loss.\n\n**What reduces the risk?** Require strong multi-factor authentication for privileged and remote access, remove unused access, minimise sensitive data retained, and monitor for unusual access or large transfers. Verify these controls with evidence. For example, check how MFA is enforced for an administrator and whether an alert is reviewed. These are defensive lessons from the scenario, not a claim that one control would have prevented the incident.\n\n[Source: Australian Information Commissioner’s concise statement](https://www.oaic.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0025/221974/Australian-Information-Commissioner-v-Medibank-Private-Limited-concise-statement.pdf)","takeaways":["Start with the asset and the people or service that could be affected.","Separate a potential consequence from an outcome supported by evidence.","Choose a control you can verify."]}',8,1,'published'),
('fundamentals','cia-triad','Cybersecurity fundamentals','shortQuiz','Identify an example of a control.','{"recap":{"summary":["A useful finding links an asset, a weakness, a plausible event and a business consequence.","A recommendation should identify an owner and describe how to verify that the risk was reduced."],"roles":[{"title":"Developer","actions":["Require sign-in before showing customer data. Check on the server that the user''s role permits the action, even if the page already hides that action.","Write a test showing an ordinary user cannot see or change an administrator-only record."]},{"title":"Infrastructure team","actions":["Keep an inventory of the services holding customer data. Assign an owner to patching, backups and monitoring for each service.","Check that backup and recovery jobs are monitored and that a recent restore test exists."]},{"title":"Business owner","actions":["Name the staff who actually need access to customer records and designate someone to approve access changes.","When a staff member leaves, remove access to shared files and applications and record when it was done."]}],"assurance":["Turn a vague finding like “sharing is insecure” into an evidence-backed finding. Record the observed setting, who could be affected, the likely consequence, the agreed owner and how you will retest it."]}}',4,2,'published'),
('cia','cia-triad','Confidentiality, integrity and availability','lesson','Classify security impacts.',
  '{"objectives":["Classify consequences using confidentiality, integrity and availability.","Choose a control and identify evidence that it works."],"markdown":"## Metaphor: a shared booking diary\n\nA clinic keeps patient bookings in a diary. **Confidentiality** means only authorised people can read it. **Integrity** means dates and details are accurate. **Availability** means staff can retrieve it when they need to manage appointments. A locked cupboard can help with confidentiality, but it would hurt availability if nobody on duty could open it.\n\nThe three security objectives are defined in [NIST FIPS 199, Security Objectives](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/199/final).\n\n## Fictional scenarios: three effects on a booking service\n\n| Objective | Everyday example | What can happen | How to reduce the risk |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Confidentiality | A customer list is shared with an external recipient. | Private details could be misused or shared further. | Limit access and review sharing links regularly. |\n| Integrity | An accidental edit changes a booking from Tuesday to Thursday. | The customer misses the appointment and staff waste time resolving it. | Limit edit rights and keep version history. |\n| Availability | The booking calendar is unavailable on a busy day. | Staff must reschedule visits or make decisions without timely information. | Test recovery from backup and practise a fallback process. |\n\n## True incident: WannaCry and the NHS, England, 2017\n\nIn May 2017, WannaCry disrupted NHS organisations in England. The National Audit Office reported that thousands of appointments and operations were cancelled. NHS England estimated that more than **19,000 appointments** were cancelled overall, based on the data it had. In five areas, patients had to travel farther for emergency care. The primary consequence in this example is **availability**. The fact that a service was disrupted does not establish that patient data was stolen.\n\n**What reduces the risk?** Keep a device inventory, set deadlines for critical updates and verify patch coverage. Use network separation to limit disruption across services, test backup recovery and maintain a rehearsed fallback for critical work. A backup job marked successful is useful evidence, but a timed restore test tells you whether the service can actually recover.\n\n[Source: UK National Audit Office investigation](https://www.nao.org.uk/reports/investigation-wannacry-cyber-attack-and-the-nhs/)","takeaways":["Classify a finding by its consequence. One event can affect more than one objective.","Link recommendations to the consequence they reduce.","Ask for evidence that a control works in practice."]}',8,3,'published'),
('cia-check','cia-triad','Understanding the CIA triad','shortQuiz','Match an incident to its impact.','{"recap":{"summary":["Confidentiality keeps information from people who should not see it. Integrity keeps it accurate. Availability keeps it usable when needed.","Classify a finding by its observed or plausible effect. A service outage alone does not prove a data breach."],"roles":[{"title":"Developer","actions":["Protect confidentiality with sign-in and server-side role checks. Test that staff can view only the bookings permitted for their role.","Protect integrity by validating changes and recording who edited sensitive booking fields and when."]},{"title":"Infrastructure team","actions":["Agree how much data the business can afford to lose and how long it can be offline. Use those limits to set backup frequency and recovery targets.","Restore a backup to an isolated destination. Check that the restored records are usable and record the time it took."]},{"title":"Business owner","actions":["Review who has access to the booking system. Remove former staff promptly and have someone verify the removal.","Agree on a fallback booking process and assign an owner to practise it with staff."]}],"assurance":["For each CIA objective, ask for a different piece of evidence. For example, check a role test for confidentiality, review an edit record for integrity, and inspect a timed restore for availability."]}}',5,4,'published'),
('risk-walkthrough','cia-triad','Identify security risks in a fictional small business','guidedLab','Build a short risk register for a bakery.',
  '{"objective":"Record two evidence-based risks in a fictional bakery.","prerequisites":["Read the CIA triad lesson"],"tools":["Text editor or spreadsheet"],"setup":"Create a risk register with these columns: Evidence, asset, what could happen, CIA impact, recommended control, verification.","safety":"Use only the fictional information supplied in the lab.","steps":[{"id":"assets","title":"List assets","instructions":"**Fictional scenario: a bakery.** The bakery records customer names, contact numbers, order details and due dates in one shared spreadsheet. Identify the key information asset and explain why it matters.","expectedResult":"A named asset and a sentence about the business process it supports.","hint":"Ask what the bakery would struggle to make or deliver if the spreadsheet were missing or wrong.","workedAnswer":"**Asset:** Customer order and delivery information. Staff use it to prepare the right items by the due date and contact customers if an order changes."},{"id":"risks","title":"Record risks and controls","instructions":"Anyone on staff can edit every order. The owner says backups run, but has never tested a restore. Record one risk for each observation. Suggest a control and describe how you would verify it.","expectedResult":"Two entries with evidence, business consequence, CIA impact, a control and a verification step.","hint":"A wrong due date is an accuracy problem. An order list you cannot recover is an access problem. Start each statement with the evidence you were given.","workedAnswer":"| Evidence | What could happen | CIA impact | Control | Verify it |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| All staff can change all orders. | An accidental edit could change a due date and cause a missed delivery. | Integrity | Limit edits to staff who maintain orders and enable version history. | Review editing permissions and recover a test cell from version history. |\n| No restore has been tested. | A lost file could leave staff without an order list during trading. | Availability | Schedule a restore test and record how long recovery takes. | Restore a fictional copy and verify that order details and dates are intact. |"}],"troubleshooting":"If a consequence is unclear, name the task the bakery would be unable to do. If an observation is unverified, record it as a question to investigate."}',18,5,'published'),
('risk-review','cia-triad','Assess a fictional business scenario','independentLab','Write a short assessment for a bicycle repair shop.',
  '{"scenario":"**Fictional scenario: Harbour Cycle Repairs.** Harbour Cycle Repairs uses a booking calendar shared with former staff. Its exported backup has never been restored.","objectives":["Prioritise practical improvements"],"tools":["Text editor or spreadsheet"],"tasks":["Identify two risks","Recommend one control per risk","Choose the first action and justify it"],"deliverables":["A two-row risk register and a short recommendation"],"checklist":[{"id":"evidence","label":"Each risk cites supplied evidence"},{"id":"priority","label":"The recommendation explains priority"}]}',22,6,'published'),
('chapter-check','cia-triad','Final chapter quiz','finalQuiz','Apply foundational security concepts to a business scenario.','{"recap":{"summary":["A sound assessment moves from observed evidence to a plausible consequence, then to a control that addresses it.","In the lab, excess editing permissions threatened accuracy, while an untested restore threatened availability. Former staff access was a confidentiality concern."],"roles":[{"title":"Developer","actions":["Require sign-in for private records and enforce role and record-level access on the server. Test an ordinary user, an administrator and a revoked account.","Validate edits and keep an audit trail for sensitive changes such as due dates. Check that a reviewer can identify who acted and what changed."]},{"title":"Infrastructure team","actions":["Agree recovery time and acceptable data loss with the business owner. Keep backups isolated from the main service and secure backup access.","Run a timed restore with representative data, verify record accuracy, and track any failures to an owner and due date."]},{"title":"Business owner","actions":["Approve the recovery targets and fallback process. Assign an owner and a due date to each agreed improvement."]},{"title":"HR","actions":["Notify IT and application owners when a worker leaves or changes roles. Confirm the effective date and record that access removal was acknowledged."]}],"assurance":["Ask each team for evidence, then document what you verified, what remains uncertain, the business impact and a prioritised recommendation. Follow up with a specific retest. This is the distinction between listing controls and providing assurance."]}}',10,7,'published');

